<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cypselus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 657–627 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cypselus was the first tyrant of Corinth, ruling from about 657 to 627 BCE after overthrowing the aristocratic Bacchiad clan. He and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periander">Periander</a>, of the Cypselid dynasty, founded colonies in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, including Ambracia, Leucas and Anactorium, governed by members of the family. The dynasty fell soon after Periander's death, around 585 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek aristocracies were challenged first by tyrants, such as <b>Cypselus</b>, who overthrew the Bacchiadae around 657 BCE, and then by democracy, but rule by the few survived in many cities, and Sparta kept its Gerousia, a council of twenty-eight elders over sixty and the two kings.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>At Corinth the Bacchiad clan ruled through a magistrate chosen yearly from its own ranks until <b>Cypselus</b> overthrew it around 657 BCE; Herodotus called their regime an oligarchy and noted that they married only among themselves.</blockquote><a href="/government/oligarchy">Oligarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…Corinth the Bacchiadae, a single clan that married only among themselves and governed the city, were overthrown by <b>Cypselus</b>, traditionally in 657 BCE, though some historians prefer a date several decades later; he killed or exiled members of the clan and ruled, it was said, without a bodyguard.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greek word tyranny first described how power had been won, not how it was used: <b>Cypselus</b> became tyrant of Corinth by overthrowing the Bacchiad clan, traditionally in 657 BCE, and some tyrants, such as Peisistratus at Athens, were remembered for good government.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote>Ambracia was founded c. 625 BCE by Corinthians under Gorgus, a son of the tyrant <b>Cypselus</b>, on the Arachthos river north of the Ambracian Gulf.</blockquote><a href="/state/ambracia">Ambracia</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cypselus</b> overthrew the Bacchiads c. 657 BCE, and his son Periander made Corinth a major power.</blockquote><a href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a></li></ul></section>
